# Developed in tandem with samples/GCP/ProcessEngineering's own Create_Doc_Agent
# (process_agents/common/doc_creation_agent.py), a standalone 2-stage
# action -- regenerate the flow/architecture diagram, then the final Word
# document -- from whatever process or design JSON is ALREADY saved on
# disk. NO re-analysis, NO design regeneration, NO compliance/simulation
# review, and the saved JSON itself is never modified. This is
# deliberately NOT process.hocon's Process_Pipeline / design.hocon's
# Design_Doc_Pipeline run with its early steps skipped -- those networks
# have no entry point that starts midway through, and forcing a "just
# regenerate" request through either of them would re-run the full
# generate/review cycle, exactly what this network exists to avoid.
#
# Domain-agnostic by design, matching the ADK port's own single
# Create_Doc_Agent (its own router sends BOTH "regenerate the process
# document" and "regenerate the design document" here). When the user's
# own request names which one they mean, Regenerate_Document_Agent passes
# that through explicitly as schema_type -- only a genuinely generic
# "regenerate the document" falls back to auto-detecting from whichever
# JSON file is actually on disk (generate_clean_diagram/
# create_standard_doc_from_file's own detect_schema_type_from_disk),
# which is correct only when at most one of the two files exists: it
# always resolves to "process" whenever both do, confirmed directly to
# silently regenerate the wrong document otherwise.
{
    include "config/llm_config.hocon",

    "metadata": {
        "description": "Regenerates the current process/design/requirements/proposal-response/test-specification document (and, for process/design, its flow diagram) from whatever is already saved on disk -- no re-analysis, no compliance/simulation review, just re-rendering the existing content.",
        "tags": ["process", "design", "requirements", "proposal-response", "test-specification", "process-engineering-port"],
        "sample_queries": [
            "Please regenerate the process document.",
            "Regenerate the design document.",
            "Regenerate the requirements document.",
            "Regenerate the proposal response document.",
            "Regenerate the test specification document."
        ]
    },

    "tools": [
        {
            "name": "Regenerate_Document_Agent",

            "function": {
                "description": """
I regenerate ONLY the final Word document (and, for process/design, its flow/architecture diagram)
for the current process, design, requirements summary, proposal response, or test specification --
from whatever is already saved on disk. I never re-run analysis, design generation, or
compliance/simulation review, and I never change the saved content itself. Tell me to regenerate
the document.
""",
                "parameters": {
                    "type": "object",
                    "properties": {
                        "inquiry": {"type": "string", "description": "The user's regenerate-document request."}
                    },
                    "required": ["inquiry"]
                }
            },

            "instructions": """
# ROLE
You regenerate ONLY the flow/architecture diagram and the final Word document from whatever process
or design JSON is already saved on disk. You NEVER re-run analysis, design generation, compliance
review, or simulation, and you NEVER modify the saved JSON itself -- that is every other network's
job, on a separate request.

# DETERMINE schema_type FIRST
Before calling either tool, check whether "inquiry" itself names a domain:
- If it explicitly says "process" / "process document" / "the process", schema_type = "process".
- If it explicitly says "design" / "design document" / "HLD" / "LLD" / "architecture document" /
  "the design", schema_type = "design".
- If it explicitly says "requirements" / "requirements document" / "the requirements",
  schema_type = "requirements". UNLIKE "process"/"design", this one is NEVER auto-detected -- the
  saved requirements summary is read as context by other pipelines far more often than it's the
  thing someone actually wants rendered, so there is no generic fallback for it at all. Only an
  explicit "requirements" mention reaches this branch.
- If it explicitly says "proposal response" / "response to the proposal" / "the proposal response",
  schema_type = "proposal_response" -- same reasoning as "requirements", NEVER auto-detected.
- If it explicitly says "test specification" / "test cases document" / "the test specification",
  schema_type = "test_specification" -- same reasoning as "requirements", NEVER auto-detected.
  UNLIKE "requirements"/"proposal_response", the saved test_specification.json DOES have its own
  "name" field (set by Test_Specification_Agent), so no fallback title needs inventing for it.
- Otherwise (a generic "regenerate the document" with no domain named), leave schema_type unset --
  do NOT guess. This case falls back to whichever single file actually exists on disk (process or
  design only -- never requirements/proposal_response/test_specification); if BOTH a process and a
  design document exist and the request is this generic, that is a genuinely ambiguous request --
  regenerate whichever the most recent turns in this conversation were about, or if that's unclear
  too, ask the user to say "the process document" or "the design document".

# DETERMINE design_variant (ONLY relevant when schema_type is, or will resolve to, "design")
The High-Level Design and Low-Level Design are always SEPARATE documents (never one combined file)
-- check whether "inquiry" names which one it wants:
- If it explicitly asks for only the HLD / High-Level Design (and does not also mention the
  LLD/Low-Level Design or "combined"/"both"), design_variant = "hld".
- If it explicitly asks for only the LLD / Low-Level Design (and does not also mention the
  HLD/High-Level Design or "combined"/"both"), design_variant = "lld".
- Otherwise -- it asks for "the design document(s)", "HLD and LLD", "combined", "both", or simply
  doesn't specify -- leave design_variant unset (regenerates whichever of the two the saved design
  data actually supports; typically both).
This determination is meaningless for every other schema_type -- do not set design_variant at all
when schema_type is "process"/"requirements"/"proposal_response"/"test_specification".

# SEQUENCE
1. If schema_type is "requirements", "proposal_response", or "test_specification", SKIP this step
   entirely -- none of the three has a flow/architecture diagram to regenerate -- and go straight to
   step 2. Otherwise, call regenerate_flow_diagram, passing {"schema_type": ...} if you determined
   one above (omit the argument entirely otherwise). ALWAYS produces some diagram, even a minimal
   placeholder when nothing real is saved yet for that domain -- so its own result alone does NOT
   tell you whether a real document exists to regenerate. Always proceed to step 2 regardless of
   what this returns.
2. Call regenerate_document with the SAME schema_type determination, plus design_variant if you
   determined one above and schema_type is "design" (no "process_name" argument needed -- the saved
   JSON's own name/title takes precedence over the fallback, except for
   "requirements"/"proposal_response", neither of which has a title field at all -- pass a
   reasonable title yourself, e.g. "<industry_sector> Requirements Specification" or
   "<industry_sector> Proposal Response" if the loaded data names a sector, else "Requirements
   Specification"/"Proposal Response". "test_specification" DOES have its own "name" field, so no
   fallback title is needed for it -- pass "process_name" only as a harmless default if you have
   nothing better). This is the real check: if its result starts with "ERROR", there is no actual
   saved document of that type yet -- reply that there is nothing to regenerate, and that they
   should create one first (e.g. "create a process for ..." / "create a design document for ..." /
   "read the files in <directory> and summarize the requirements" / "respond to this proposal ..." /
   "create a test specification for the saved requirements"). If it starts with "SUCCESS", report
   that the document was regenerated from the current saved content (and, for process/design, that
   the flow diagram was too) -- for "design", the SUCCESS text itself names which document(s) were
   actually produced ("...saved at <path>" for one, "...saved -- HLD: <path>; LLD: <path>" for
   both); relay that, don't assume both always exist.

Plain prose only in your final reply -- no raw JSON, tool names, or internal field names.
""",
            "tools": ["regenerate_flow_diagram", "regenerate_document"]
        },

        {
            "name": "regenerate_flow_diagram",
            "function": {
                "description": "Regenerates the flow/architecture diagram from whichever process/design JSON is already saved on disk.",
                "parameters": {
                    "type": "object",
                    "properties": {
                        "schema_type": {
                            "type": "string",
                            "enum": ["process", "design"],
                            "description": "Pass this when the user's own request names which document they mean. Omit it only for a genuinely generic request -- it then auto-detects from whichever single file exists on disk, which is ambiguous whenever both do."
                        }
                    }
                }
            },
            "class": "edge_inference_tool.RegenerateFlowDiagramCodedTool"
        },
        {
            "name": "regenerate_document",
            "function": {
                "description": "Regenerates the final Word document from whichever process/design/requirements/proposal-response/test-specification JSON is already saved on disk.",
                "parameters": {
                    "type": "object",
                    "properties": {
                        "schema_type": {
                            "type": "string",
                            "enum": ["process", "design", "requirements", "proposal_response", "test_specification"],
                            "description": "Pass this when the user's own request names which document they mean. \"requirements\"/\"proposal_response\"/\"test_specification\" must ALWAYS be passed explicitly -- none of the three is ever auto-detected. Omit this entirely only for a genuinely generic process/design request -- it then auto-detects from whichever single file exists on disk, which is ambiguous whenever both do."
                        },
                        "process_name": {
                            "type": "string",
                            "description": "Optional fallback document title, used only if the saved JSON itself has no name/title (always required in practice for schema_type=\"requirements\"/\"proposal_response\", neither of which has a title field at all; test_specification has its own \"name\" field so this is just a harmless default for it)."
                        },
                        "design_variant": {
                            "type": "string",
                            "enum": ["hld", "lld", "both"],
                            "description": "Only meaningful when schema_type is \"design\". Which of the two separate design documents to regenerate -- the High-Level Design, the Low-Level Design, or both. Omit to regenerate both (or whichever of the two the current design data actually contains, if only one does)."
                        }
                    }
                }
            },
            "class": "doc_generation_tool.RegenerateDocumentCodedTool"
        }
    ]
}
